  <MedicalNote />

  <p>If you're meticulously tracking your diet, one of the most common questions you'll ask is: <strong>how many calories are in a gram of protein?</strong> Understanding the relationship between protein grams and total calories is the foundation of nutrition, whether you're using a <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> to build muscle or a <a href="/weight-loss-calculator/">weight loss calculator</a> to shed fat.</p>

  <p>In this comprehensive guide, we will break down the exact math behind <strong>protein to calories</strong>, provide rapid conversion charts for common protein targets (like 100g, 150g, and 170g), and explain the fascinating science of how your body actually burns calories just to digest protein.</p>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/protein-structure.svg" alt="Illustration showing that 1 gram of protein equals 4 calories." />

  <h2>The Core Rule: Calories per Gram of Protein</h2>
  <p>In nutritional science (based on the classic Atwater system), the energy density of macronutrients is standardized. Here is the golden rule you need to memorize:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1 gram of protein = 4 calories (kcal)</strong></li>
    <li>1 gram of carbohydrates = 4 calories</li>
    <li>1 gram of fat = 9 calories</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p>This means if you want to know <strong>how many kcals are in protein</strong>, you simply take the total grams of protein and multiply it by four.</p>

  <div class="bg-canvas-soft border-l-4 border-brand p-5 my-8 rounded-r-lg">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">The Protein Conversion Formula</h3>
    <p class="mb-0 text-lg font-mono"><code>Total Protein Calories = Protein (in grams) × 4</code></p>
  </div>

