  <h2>What is a Good Protein to Calorie Ratio? (The 10-to-1 Rule)</h2>
  <p>When you are shopping at the grocery store, you might wonder how to spot a genuinely "high protein" food versus a clever marketing gimmick. This brings up a highly searched question: <strong>what is a good ratio of protein to calories?</strong></p>
  <p>Fitness experts and nutritionists often recommend the <strong>10 to 1 calorie to protein ratio</strong>. Here is how it works:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Drop the last zero from the total calories of the food item.</li>
    <li>The remaining number is the <em>minimum</em> amount of protein (in grams) the food should have to be considered a highly efficient protein source.</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> If a cup of Greek yogurt has 120 calories, drop the zero (12). Does it have at least 12 grams of protein? If it has 15g, it surpasses the 10-to-1 rule. It is a fantastic protein source!</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> A protein bar has 250 calories. It should have at least 25g of protein. If it only has 10g, it is essentially a candy bar, heavily weighted toward carbs and fats.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Keeping an eye on this <strong>calorie to protein ratio</strong> makes hitting your targets in our <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a> infinitely easier.</p>

  <h2>How Many Calories Does it Take to Digest a Gram of Protein?</h2>
  <p>Here is where protein becomes the ultimate "cheat code" for weight loss. Not all calories are treated equally by your body. When you eat food, your digestive system has to expend energy (burn calories) just to break that food down, absorb the nutrients, and turn them into usable energy.</p>
  <p>This is known as the <strong>Thermic Effect of Food (TEF)</strong>. Protein has the highest TEF of any macronutrient:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fat TEF:</strong> 0–3%</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates TEF:</strong> 5–10%</li>
    <li><strong>Protein TEF:</strong> 20–30%</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What does this mean in practice? If you eat 100 calories of pure protein (25 grams), your body will burn 20 to 30 of those calories simply digesting it. You only net about 70 to 80 usable calories. Therefore, <strong>1 gram of protein (4 calories) actually burns roughly 0.8 to 1.2 calories during digestion</strong>.</p>
  <p>This is why high-protein diets are universally recommended in our <a href="/guides/weight-loss-guide/">weight loss guide</a>—they literally boost your metabolism.</p>

  <h2>Balancing Protein within Your Overall Calories</h2>
  <p>While protein is vital, you cannot eat unlimited amounts of it. Your <strong>protein kcal</strong> must fit within your <a href="/tdee-calculator/">Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)</a>. If you need 2,000 calories a day to maintain your weight, and you eat 150g of protein (600 calories), you have 1,400 calories left over to split between carbohydrates and fats.</p>
  
  <p>If you are struggling to figure out how to balance these numbers, don't guess. Use our suite of free tools to map out your perfect diet plan:</p>
  <ul>
